## Why This Is Asked
A practical, low‑level fairness mechanism relevant to high‑throughput systems.

## Key Concepts
- Atomic operations and memory ordering
- FIFO fairness and starvation avoidance
- Cache alignment and false sharing
- Busy-wait semantics and platform hints

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct {
  alignas(64) atomic_uint64_t next;
  alignas(64) atomic_uint64_t grant;
} ticket_lock;

void lock_init(ticket_lock *l){
  atomic_init(&l->next, 0);
  atomic_init(&l->grant, 0);
}
uint64_t lock_acquire(ticket_lock *l){
  uint64_t t = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&l->next, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  while (atomic_load_explicit(&l->grant, memory_order_acquire) != t) {
    // pause hint
  }
  return t;
}
void lock_release(ticket_lock *l, uint64_t t){
  atomic_store_explicit(&l->grant, t+1, memory_order_release);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assess understanding of low-level synchronization, atomic operations, and memory ordering in a practical, portable way.

## Key Concepts

- Spinlock using atomic_flag
- Memory ordering: acquire on lock, release on unlock
- Exponential backoff with PAUSE to reduce contention
- Correctness test with concurrent increments

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <immintrin.h>

typedef struct { atomic_flag flag; } spinlock;

static inline void spinlock_init(spinlock* s) {
  atomic_flag_clear(&s->flag);
}

static inline void spin_lock(spinlock* s) {
  while (atomic_flag_test_and_set_explicit(&s->flag, memory_order_acquire)) {
    _mm_pause();
    // small exponential backoff
    for (int b = 1; b < 1024; b <<= 1) {
      _mm_pause();
    }
  }
}

static inline void spin_unlock(spinlock* s) {
  atomic_flag_clear_explicit(&s->flag, memory_order_release);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for NUMA systems to reduce cross-socket traffic?
- What are the trade-offs of using a ticket-lock vs this spinlock in high-contention workloads?

